// Domain 2: AI Strategy and Business Value Creation (28%)
window.QUESTIONS = (window.QUESTIONS || []).concat([
{ id: "d2-01", d: 2,
  q: "A company's CEO notes that several competitors have announced generative AI (GenAI) initiatives. The CEO asks the leadership team to start a GenAI project this quarter. No business problem has been identified yet.\n\nWhich step should the company take FIRST?",
  o: ["Identify the business problems and measurable outcomes that matter most.",
      "Select the most capable foundation model, and start a proof of concept.",
      "Hire a team of data scientists to explore possible GenAI use cases.",
      "Replicate the GenAI use case that the largest competitor announced publicly."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AI is a means to a business outcome, not an objective in itself. The company has not identified a business problem. Defining the problems and measurable outcomes first lets the company decide where GenAI is appropriate and how to measure success.",
      "Selecting a model first is a technology-first approach. Without a defined problem, the company cannot evaluate whether the model or the proof of concept creates value.",
      "Hiring a team before defining use cases commits budget without a clear objective. The team would also lack criteria to decide which use cases matter.",
      "A competitor's use case reflects that competitor's strategy, data, and customers. Competitive pressure is useful context, but investment should rest on the company's own business value and feasibility."] },

{ id: "d2-02", d: 2,
  q: "A contact center deploys a generative AI (GenAI) tool that drafts replies for agents. The goal is to reduce operating cost per contact without lowering service quality. Leadership wants key performance indicators (KPIs) that show whether the tool meets this goal.\n\nWhich set of KPIs should the company track?",
  o: ["Number of prompts sent to the tool, tokens used per day, and average model response latency",
      "Cost per contact, average handle time, and customer satisfaction, each against a baseline",
      "Agent headcount, total contact center budget, and number of tool licenses purchased",
      "Number of agents trained on the tool, login frequency, and weekly active users"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Prompt counts, token usage, and latency are technical and usage metrics. They help manage the tool but do not show whether cost per contact fell or quality held.",
      "Cost per contact measures the cost goal directly. Average handle time shows the efficiency driver. Customer satisfaction confirms that quality did not drop. Comparing each KPI with a pre-launch baseline shows the tool's impact.",
      "Headcount, budget, and licenses are organizational inputs. They do not measure the outcome of each contact or its quality.",
      "Training and login metrics are adoption indicators. They can predict success but do not show whether the tool reduced cost per contact or maintained quality."] },

{ id: "d2-03", d: 2,
  q: "A company plans a 12-week pilot of an AI tool that drafts responses to customer emails. Leadership will decide whether to scale the tool based on its measured impact.\n\nWhat should the company do BEFORE the pilot starts?",
  o: ["Measure current time per email, email volume, quality score, and cost per email.",
      "Estimate pre-pilot performance at the end of the pilot, based on staff recollection.",
      "Publish the savings that leadership expects so that staff understand the pilot goals.",
      "Choose the most accurate model available so that the pilot shows the largest impact."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Baseline metrics capture current performance before a change. Without a baseline, the company cannot quantify the tool's impact. Measuring time, volume, quality, and cost before the pilot provides a before-and-after comparison.",
      "Recollections after the fact are unreliable and can be biased by the pilot's results. The baseline must be measured before the pilot starts.",
      "Publishing expected savings sets expectations but does not create a measurement point. It can also bias how staff report results.",
      "Model choice affects the pilot's results, not the company's ability to measure them. Without a baseline, even a strong result cannot be quantified."] },

{ id: "d2-04", d: 2,
  q: "An AI initiative requires a total investment of $200,000. The company expects the initiative to generate $300,000 in benefits over the evaluation period.\n\nWhat is the return on investment (ROI) of the initiative?",
  o: ["33%", "50%", "150%", "67%"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["33% is the net benefit of $100,000 divided by the total benefit of $300,000. ROI divides the net benefit by the investment, not by the benefit.",
      "ROI equals the net benefit divided by the investment. The net benefit is $300,000 minus $200,000, which is $100,000. $100,000 divided by $200,000 is 50%.",
      "150% is the total benefit divided by the investment. That figure is a benefit-to-cost ratio, not ROI.",
      "67% is the investment divided by the total benefit. This calculation does not measure return."] },

{ id: "d2-05", d: 2,
  q: "An AI solution costs $480,000 to implement. Once live, the solution is expected to save $40,000 each month. Ignore ongoing costs for this calculation.\n\nWhat is the approximate payback period?",
  o: ["6 months", "12 months", "18 months", "40 months"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Six months of savings total $240,000, which recovers only half of the $480,000 investment.",
      "The payback period is the investment divided by the periodic net benefit. $480,000 divided by $40,000 each month is 12 months. A real business case would also subtract ongoing costs, such as inference and support, from the monthly savings.",
      "Eighteen months of savings total $720,000, which exceeds the investment. The investment is recovered earlier, at 12 months.",
      "40 is the monthly savings in thousands of dollars, not a number of months. It is not a payback calculation."] },

{ id: "d2-06", d: 2,
  q: "A company rolls out a generative AI (GenAI) productivity assistant to 5,000 employees. After one month, leadership wants early signals that predict whether the assistant will deliver its annual productivity target.\n\nWhich metrics are leading indicators? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Weekly active usage among licensed employees",
      "Completion rate of the AI literacy training",
      "Annual reduction in operating costs",
      "Year-over-year revenue growth per employee",
      "Full-year employee productivity survey score"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Leading indicators move early and predict later outcomes. Active usage shows whether employees adopt the assistant, which must happen before productivity gains appear.",
      "Training completion predicts whether employees can use the assistant well. It is available within weeks of launch, so it is a leading indicator.",
      "Annual cost reduction is a realized outcome that is measured after the fact. It confirms results rather than predicting them.",
      "Revenue growth is measured over a full year and reflects many factors. It is an outcome measure, not an early signal.",
      "A full-year survey score arrives at the end of the year. It measures the outcome, not an early signal of it."] },

{ id: "d2-07", d: 2,
  q: "An insurer rolled out an AI claims assistant to its adjusters six weeks ago. The initiative's target is a 15% reduction in cost per claim by year end. Leadership wants an early signal of whether the target is on track.\n\nWhich metric is the BEST leading indicator?",
  o: ["The reduction in cost per claim across all regions, reported at year end",
      "The share of eligible claims that adjusters process with the assistant",
      "The total number of assistant licenses that the insurer purchased",
      "The number of parameters in the model that powers the assistant"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Year-end cost per claim is the outcome that the target measures. It confirms results after the fact, so it cannot provide an early signal.",
      "Leading indicators predict future outcomes. Cost savings depend on adjusters actually using the assistant on eligible claims. A rising share of claims processed with the assistant is an early signal that savings will follow.",
      "Licenses purchased measure spending, not use. Unused licenses do not reduce cost per claim.",
      "Model size says nothing about whether adjusters use the assistant or whether costs will fall."] },

{ id: "d2-08", d: 2,
  q: "A company drafts a business case for an AI initiative. The finance team asks the company to separate tangible benefits from intangible benefits.\n\nWhich benefit is intangible?",
  o: ["A 20% reduction in invoice processing cost",
      "Improved customer satisfaction and employee productivity",
      "$2 million in new annual revenue from AI-driven cross-selling",
      "A 10% reduction in outsourced call center spending"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Cost reduction is a tangible benefit because it translates directly into financial terms.",
      "Customer satisfaction and employee productivity are intangible benefits. They are real, but they are harder to convert directly into financial terms. The AIB-C01 exam guide uses these two as its examples of intangible benefits.",
      "Revenue growth is a tangible benefit because it is measured directly in money.",
      "A reduction in spending is a tangible benefit because it is measured directly in money."] },

{ id: "d2-09", d: 2,
  q: "A finance leader reviews an AI business case. The cost section includes only the model's price per token. The finance leader wants the business case to reflect total cost of ownership (TCO).\n\nWhich additional costs should the business case include? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Data preparation, integration, monitoring, and maintenance",
      "Change management, training, and governance activities",
      "Sunk costs from an AI project that the company canceled last year",
      "The revenue that the company expects the initiative to generate",
      "Costs of other departments' unrelated software subscriptions"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["TCO includes the full cost of running a solution over its life. Preparing and integrating data, and monitoring and maintaining the solution, are ongoing costs that token pricing does not capture.",
      "People and process costs are part of TCO. Training users, managing change, and running governance reviews all require budget.",
      "Sunk costs from a canceled project cannot be recovered and do not change with this decision. A forward-looking business case should exclude them.",
      "Expected revenue is a benefit, not a cost. It belongs in the benefits section of the business case.",
      "Unrelated subscriptions are not caused by this initiative. Including them would overstate the initiative's cost."] },

{ id: "d2-10", d: 2,
  q: "A company has 12 proposed AI use cases and budget for three. Business unit leaders each argue for their own proposals. The executive team wants a defensible way to choose.\n\nWhich approach should the company use to prioritize the use cases?",
  o: ["Score each use case on value, feasibility, sustainability, strategic alignment, and risk.",
      "Fund the three use cases whose sponsors present the most compelling business cases.",
      "Fund the three use cases that apply the newest AI techniques available to the company.",
      "Fund the three use cases with the lowest cost so that the budget covers more experiments."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A structured scoring framework compares use cases on the same criteria. The exam guide names business value, feasibility, sustainability, and strategic alignment as prioritization factors. Adding risk makes the comparison complete and the choice defensible.",
      "A compelling presentation reflects the sponsor's persuasiveness, not necessarily the use case's value. Without common criteria, the choice is not defensible.",
      "New techniques do not guarantee business value. This approach is technology-first and can select use cases that are hard to deliver.",
      "Low cost alone ignores value and strategic fit. Cheap use cases can deliver little impact while higher-value use cases go unfunded."] },

{ id: "d2-11", d: 2,
  q: "A proposed AI use case scores high on business value. The use case scores low on feasibility because the required data is fragmented across systems and has poor quality. Other proposed use cases score high on both value and feasibility.\n\nWhat should the company do with the high-value, low-feasibility use case?",
  o: ["Keep it on the roadmap, fix the data foundations, and deliver the feasible use cases now.",
      "Launch it into production now, because its business value outweighs its feasibility issues.",
      "Cancel it permanently, and remove it from all future planning cycles and roadmaps.",
      "Train the model on synthetic data instead, and deploy it without further data work."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["High value makes the use case strategically important, but low feasibility means it is not ready. Improving data quality and integration closes the gap. Delivering the feasible use cases meanwhile builds momentum and capability.",
      "Launching without usable data risks a failed project. The data problems would limit the model's accuracy regardless of the use case's value.",
      "The use case has high business value, and its blocker can be fixed. Canceling it permanently discards a valuable opportunity.",
      "Synthetic data can supplement real data, but it cannot replace an accurate view of the business. Deploying without validation adds risk instead of fixing the data problem."] },

{ id: "d2-12", d: 2,
  q: "A company needs an AI capability that is central to its competitive differentiation. The capability depends on the company's proprietary data and requires deep customization. The company has a strong in-house machine learning (ML) team.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Build the capability in-house",
      "Buy an off-the-shelf software as a service (SaaS) product",
      "Partner with a consultancy that owns the resulting IP",
      "Wait until a vendor releases the exact feature"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Building fits when a capability is strategic and differentiating, depends on proprietary data, needs deep customization, and internal expertise exists. All four conditions apply. The tradeoffs are higher cost, a longer timeline, and ongoing maintenance.",
      "Off-the-shelf products suit common, non-differentiating needs. They offer limited customization, and competitors can buy the same product.",
      "Partnering can add expertise. However, the company already has a strong team, and letting a partner own the intellectual property (IP) gives away control of a differentiating capability.",
      "Waiting delays a strategic capability and assumes that a vendor will build it. A vendor feature would also be available to competitors."] },

{ id: "d2-13", d: 2,
  q: "A mid-sized company wants AI meeting summarization for all employees within one month. The need is common across industries, and mature products exist. The company has little internal AI expertise.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Build a custom summarization model in-house",
      "Buy a proven commercial summarization product",
      "Partner with a research lab to develop a new model",
      "Delay the project until internal skills are in place"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Building takes longer and requires expertise that the company lacks. Summarization is a common need, so a custom model adds little differentiation.",
      "Buying fits when the need is common, mature products exist, speed matters, and internal expertise is limited. All four conditions apply. The company should still evaluate data handling, integration, and licensing costs.",
      "A research partnership suits novel problems. Meeting summarization is already solved by mature products, and research would not meet the one-month timeline.",
      "Delaying misses the one-month timeline. A commercial product removes the need for deep internal skills."] },

{ id: "d2-14", d: 2,
  q: "A regional hospital group wants an AI solution for clinical documentation. The implementation is complex and must meet strict healthcare regulations. The group lacks internal AI skills and needs the solution within six months.\n\nWhich approach is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Build the solution in-house with the current IT team",
      "Partner with a firm that has healthcare AI expertise",
      "Buy a general-purpose chatbot, and configure it internally",
      "Postpone the initiative until regulations become simpler"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["The IT team lacks AI skills and healthcare AI experience. Building in-house would risk both the timeline and regulatory compliance.",
      "Partnering fits when internal capabilities are insufficient, specialized domain and regulatory expertise is needed, and speed matters. A partner can also transfer knowledge so that the group builds its own capability over time.",
      "A general-purpose chatbot is not designed for clinical documentation or healthcare regulations. The group also lacks the skills to adapt it safely.",
      "There is no indication that regulations will become simpler. Postponing delays the value without addressing the capability gap."] },

{ id: "d2-15", d: 2,
  q: "A company is making a build, buy, or partner decision for a new AI capability. The team has already assessed its internal AI skills.\n\nWhich other factors should the decision consider? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Strategic importance and control of data and intellectual property (IP)",
      "Budget, timeline, vendor proposals, and regulatory compliance requirements",
      "How many of the company's direct competitors have chosen each option",
      "The number of AI features listed in each vendor's product roadmap",
      "Which option lets the project start fastest, regardless of total cost"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Strategic, differentiating capabilities favor building or close partnership, where the company keeps control of its data and IP. Commodity capabilities favor buying.",
      "The exam guide lists budget, timelines, capabilities, vendor proposals, and regulatory compliance requirements as build-buy-partner factors. Each can rule an option in or out.",
      "Competitors' choices reflect their own strategies and capabilities. They do not show which option fits this company's needs.",
      "A long feature roadmap does not show fit with the company's requirements. Promised features may also not be delivered.",
      "Speed matters, but ignoring total cost can lead to a poor long-term decision. The decision should weigh time to value alongside cost and risk."] },

{ id: "d2-16", d: 2,
  q: "A company decides to buy rather than build an AI capability. The company wants to discover, evaluate, and procure third-party AI software that runs on AWS. The company wants the purchase to appear on its existing AWS bill.\n\nWhich AWS offering will meet these requirements?",
  o: ["AWS Marketplace", "AWS Cost Explorer", "Amazon SageMaker AI", "AWS Pricing Calculator"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AWS Marketplace is a curated digital catalog for finding, buying, and deploying third-party software, data, and services. Purchases are billed through the customer's AWS account. The exam guide names AWS Marketplace as a tool for build-buy-partner decisions.",
      "AWS Cost Explorer analyzes actual AWS spending and usage. It does not list or sell third-party software.",
      "Amazon SageMaker AI is a service for building, training, and deploying machine learning models. It supports the build option, not procurement of third-party software.",
      "AWS Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of AWS services before deployment. It does not provide a catalog of third-party products."] },

{ id: "d2-17", d: 2,
  q: "A finance team needs to estimate the monthly AWS cost of a proposed AI solution. The solution has not been deployed yet. The team wants to compare low, expected, and high usage scenarios.\n\nWhich AWS tool should the team use?",
  o: ["AWS Cost Explorer", "AWS Pricing Calculator", "AWS Marketplace", "Amazon Bedrock Guardrails"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["AWS Cost Explorer analyzes historical and current spending for resources that already run. The solution has not been deployed, so there is no usage to analyze.",
      "AWS Pricing Calculator estimates the cost of planned AWS services before deployment. The team can model different usage levels, which supports scenario planning in a business case.",
      "AWS Marketplace is a catalog for buying third-party software. It does not estimate the cost of a planned architecture.",
      "Amazon Bedrock Guardrails applies safety controls to generative AI applications. It is not a cost estimation tool."] },

{ id: "d2-18", d: 2,
  q: "A company launched a generative AI (GenAI) application three months ago. Leadership wants to see actual spending trends by service and identify where AI costs are growing fastest.\n\nWhich AWS tool is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["AWS Pricing Calculator", "AWS Cost Explorer", "AWS Marketplace", "AWS Cloud Adoption Framework (AWS CAF)"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["AWS Pricing Calculator estimates costs before deployment. Leadership wants to analyze actual spending that has already occurred.",
      "AWS Cost Explorer visualizes actual AWS spending and usage over time, with filters by service and forecasting. It shows where costs are growing.",
      "AWS Marketplace is for discovering and buying third-party software. It does not analyze spending trends.",
      "AWS CAF is a framework for planning organizational transformation. It is not a cost analysis tool."] },

{ id: "d2-19", d: 2,
  q: "A startup is launching a new generative AI (GenAI) feature. The startup cannot predict demand. Usage might stay low or might spike unexpectedly. The startup wants to avoid upfront commitments.\n\nWhich pricing model is MOST suitable at launch?",
  o: ["Consumption-based pricing, such as paying per token or request",
      "A multi-year commitment for dedicated, reserved model capacity",
      "Seat-based licensing for every potential customer",
      "Purchased servers that host the model in the startup's own data center"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Consumption-based pricing charges only for actual usage and requires no upfront commitment. It suits uncertain or variable demand. The startup should still set budgets and monitor spending, because costs rise quickly if usage spikes.",
      "A multi-year commitment suits steady, predictable workloads. The startup cannot predict demand and wants to avoid upfront commitments.",
      "Seat-based licensing charges per user, whether or not the user is active. It does not fit an unpredictable customer base.",
      "Buying servers requires a large upfront investment and fixed capacity. Capacity could be wasted if demand stays low, or fall short if demand spikes."] },

{ id: "d2-20", d: 2,
  q: "A company's generative AI (GenAI) workload now has steady, high, and predictable volume. The application needs guaranteed throughput during business hours.\n\nWhich pricing approach is MOST likely to be cost-effective and reliable?",
  o: ["Continue with on-demand, pay-per-request pricing",
      "Committed capacity, such as provisioned throughput",
      "Seat-based licensing for each application user",
      "A free tier with request throttling during peak hours"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["On-demand pricing is flexible, but it does not guarantee throughput. At steady high volume, it can also cost more than committed capacity.",
      "Committing to dedicated capacity, such as Amazon Bedrock Provisioned Throughput or instance-based hosting, can lower unit cost at steady volume. Dedicated capacity also provides the guaranteed throughput that the application needs.",
      "Seat-based licensing charges per user. It does not provide guaranteed throughput for an application workload.",
      "Free tiers are designed for evaluation. Throttling during peak hours conflicts with the need for guaranteed throughput."] },

{ id: "d2-21", d: 2,
  q: "A company plans to give 3,000 knowledge workers an AI assistant. The vendor charges a fixed monthly fee for each user. The company wants to understand the main risk to return on investment (ROI) under this model.\n\nWhich statement BEST describes the pricing model and its key consideration?",
  o: ["Consumption-based pricing, so cost depends only on the tokens that users consume",
      "Seat-based pricing, so ROI depends on how many licensed users actually adopt it",
      "Instance-based pricing, so cost depends on the compute hours that the vendor runs",
      "Outcome-based pricing, so the vendor is paid only when productivity gains occur"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Consumption-based pricing charges for usage, such as tokens. The vendor charges a fixed fee per user, not per token.",
      "A fixed fee per user is seat-based pricing. Cost is predictable, but licenses that employees do not use return nothing. Tracking adoption and right-sizing licenses protects ROI. Amazon Quick is an example of a seat-based AI service.",
      "Instance-based pricing charges for compute capacity over time. The company pays a fixed amount per user, regardless of compute.",
      "Outcome-based pricing ties payment to results. The scenario describes a fixed monthly fee, not a payment that depends on outcomes."] },

{ id: "d2-22", d: 2,
  q: "A company wants to summarize 2 million archived documents with a generative AI (GenAI) model. The company needs the results within a week. No user is waiting for any individual summary.\n\nWhich approach is MOST cost-effective?",
  o: ["Batch inference that processes the documents asynchronously at a lower price",
      "Real-time, on-demand requests to the largest model that the company can access",
      "Provisioned throughput with a six-month commitment for this one-time project",
      "Manual summarization by contractors, with AI used only for spot checks"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Batch inference processes large volumes asynchronously and is typically priced lower than real-time inference. On Amazon Bedrock, batch inference costs about half of on-demand pricing for supported models. The job is not time-sensitive, so batch is the cost-effective choice.",
      "Real-time requests are priced for low-latency use, and the largest model raises cost without a stated quality need. No user is waiting, so real-time processing adds cost with no benefit.",
      "A six-month commitment suits steady, ongoing workloads. This is a one-time job, so the company would pay for capacity it no longer needs.",
      "Manual summarization of 2 million documents would be far slower and more expensive than batch inference."] },

{ id: "d2-23", d: 2,
  q: "A retailer and its competitors can all access the same foundation models through cloud services. The retailer wants its AI investments to create an advantage that competitors cannot easily copy.\n\nWhich source is MOST likely to create a sustainable competitive advantage?",
  o: ["Proprietary data built into the retailer's unique workflows and core processes",
      "Early access to the most popular foundation model through a cloud provider",
      "Use of the foundation model that has the largest number of parameters",
      "Frequent press releases that describe the retailer's AI initiatives"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Competitors can access the same models, so the model alone is not a lasting advantage. Proprietary data, unique workflows, and deep integration into core processes are hard to replicate. Therefore, they create a more sustainable advantage.",
      "Early access is temporary. Competitors can gain access to the same model shortly after.",
      "Model size is available to any company that pays for it. It also does not guarantee better results for a specific business.",
      "Press releases shape perception but create no capability. Competitors can issue the same announcements."] },

{ id: "d2-24", d: 2,
  q: "An insurer uses AI to analyze real-time driving data from customers who opt in. The insurer launches a new usage-based policy that prices premiums by driving behavior. The policy creates a revenue stream that did not exist before.\n\nWhich type of AI value does this initiative represent?",
  o: ["Business model transformation", "Incremental process efficiency", "Cost avoidance through automation", "IT infrastructure optimization"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Business model transformation uses AI to create new products, services, or ways to earn revenue. A usage-based policy priced from real-time data is a new offering and a new revenue stream.",
      "Process efficiency means doing existing work faster or cheaper. The initiative creates a new product, not a faster version of an existing process.",
      "Cost avoidance reduces spending. The initiative's main result is new revenue, not lower costs.",
      "Infrastructure optimization improves how IT systems run. The initiative changes what the insurer sells."] },

{ id: "d2-25", d: 2,
  q: "A 3-month generative AI (GenAI) pilot for drafting sales proposals reduced drafting time by 40%. User satisfaction is high, and costs are within budget. Governance and security reviews are complete.\n\nWhat should the company do NEXT?",
  o: ["Scale in phases, and keep measuring the results against the baseline.",
      "Roll out to every department at once, and stop tracking the results.",
      "Pause for a year until newer models become available on the market.",
      "Extend the pilot for another year with the same users and scope."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["The pilot shows value, adoption, cost control, and governance readiness, which supports scaling. A phased rollout limits risk. Continued measurement against the baseline confirms that the value holds at scale.",
      "The evidence supports scaling, but a simultaneous rollout concentrates risk. Stopping measurement removes the ability to confirm value or detect problems.",
      "Newer models do not change the pilot's evidence. Waiting a year delays proven value with no clear benefit.",
      "The pilot already answered its questions. Extending it with the same scope delays value without reducing any remaining uncertainty."] },

{ id: "d2-26", d: 2,
  q: "A generative AI (GenAI) pilot targeted a 30% reduction in processing time. The pilot achieved a 2% reduction. Users report low trust in the outputs. Projected costs exceed benefits even under optimistic assumptions.\n\nWhat should the company do?",
  o: ["Scale the solution to more users to spread fixed costs and improve return on investment (ROI).",
      "Terminate or rethink the initiative, and redirect funds to higher-value use cases.",
      "Continue the pilot indefinitely without changes to collect more data.",
      "Report only the positive user feedback to justify continued funding."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Scaling a solution that misses its target multiplies the loss. Low trust and weak results would not improve with more users.",
      "Pilots exist to reduce uncertainty. The value hypothesis failed and the economics do not work, so stopping or fundamentally rethinking the initiative is the disciplined decision. Sunk costs should not drive further investment.",
      "The pilot has already produced clear evidence. Continuing without changes spends more budget without a path to the target.",
      "Selective reporting hides the evidence that leaders need to decide. It undermines trust in the AI program."] },

{ id: "d2-27", d: 2,
  q: "A pilot delivered strong productivity gains. A legal review then found an unresolved data privacy issue in how customer data flows through the solution. Design changes can fix the issue.\n\nWhat should the company do?",
  o: ["Scale now, and address the privacy issue after customers raise complaints.",
      "Pause scaling, fix the privacy issue, and then reassess the rollout.",
      "Terminate the initiative, and stop all AI projects across the company.",
      "Scale to internal users only, and leave the data flow unchanged."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Scaling with a known privacy issue multiplies the exposure. Waiting for complaints means that customer data is already mishandled.",
      "A pause fits when value is proven but a specific, fixable risk blocks scaling. Fixing the data flow first protects customers and the company. The company can then resume with confidence.",
      "The issue is fixable, and the pilot proved value. Terminating the initiative, and every other AI project, is an overreaction.",
      "The privacy issue concerns customer data, which would still flow through the unchanged solution. Limiting the rollout to internal users does not fix it."] },

{ id: "d2-28", d: 2,
  q: "An AI team presents a business case to the CFO. The CFO has rejected earlier AI proposals because their benefits could not be verified.\n\nWhich element will MOST improve the credibility of the business case?",
  o: ["Quantified benefits tied to baseline metrics, with full costs, assumptions, and risks",
      "A product demonstration video that shows the solution working in a test environment",
      "Industry reports on AI adoption rates and estimates of the market's projected growth",
      "Testimonials from other companies that adopted solutions from the same AI vendor"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["A credible business case states the problem, the full costs, the measurable benefits against a baseline, the key assumptions, the risks, and how success will be measured. These elements let the CFO verify the claims.",
      "A demonstration shows that the solution works technically. It does not quantify business value or costs.",
      "Industry reports describe the market in general. They do not show what this project will deliver for this company.",
      "Other companies' results depend on their own data and processes. Testimonials do not verify the benefits for this company."] },

{ id: "d2-29", d: 2,
  q: "A company wants to identify high-impact generative AI (GenAI) use cases across its business functions for its first investments.\n\nWhich use cases are typical high-value areas for GenAI? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Agent assist and conversation summaries in customer operations",
      "Code generation and test creation for software development teams",
      "Fully automated approval of all payments with no human review",
      "Replacing the general ledger with a large language model (LLM)",
      "Removing legal review from all contracts that GenAI drafts"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Customer operations is one of the example areas in the exam guide. Agent assist and summaries reduce handle time and improve consistency.",
      "Software development is another example area in the exam guide. Code assistants speed up coding, documentation, and testing.",
      "Removing human review from all payments removes a key financial control. High-stakes decisions need human oversight.",
      "A general ledger needs exact, deterministic records. An LLM produces probabilistic output and is not a system of record.",
      "Contracts carry legal risk. GenAI can draft contracts, but removing legal review for all of them creates unacceptable risk."] },

{ id: "d2-30", d: 2,
  q: "A company expects foundation model (FM) prices and capabilities to keep changing. The company wants the option to switch FMs later without rebuilding its application.\n\nWhich approach BEST reduces this risk?",
  o: ["Sign a long exclusive contract with one model provider for better pricing",
      "Use a managed service that offers FMs from many providers through one API",
      "Train a proprietary FM from scratch so that no vendor is involved",
      "Delay AI adoption until FM prices and capabilities stabilize"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["An exclusive contract increases lock-in. It makes switching harder if better or cheaper FMs appear.",
      "A service that offers FMs from multiple providers through one API, such as Amazon Bedrock, makes it easier to evaluate and switch FMs as needs change. Regular evaluation against business criteria keeps the choice current.",
      "Training an FM from scratch is the most expensive and slowest option. It also leaves the company unable to benefit from improvements in commercial FMs.",
      "The market may keep changing for years. Delaying adoption gives up value without reducing lock-in risk."] },

{ id: "d2-31", d: 2,
  q: "A proposed AI use case shows a 300% return on investment (ROI). The calculation assumes that 100% of employees will adopt the tool in the first month. Similar tools in the company reached 40% adoption after six months.\n\nWhat should the strategist do before presenting the business case?",
  o: ["Present the 300% figure as-is, because it reflects the tool's full potential.",
      "Run a sensitivity analysis with realistic adoption rates, and state the assumptions.",
      "Remove adoption from the calculation, because adoption is hard to predict.",
      "Double the investment estimate so that it offsets the risk from the adoption assumption."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["The assumption conflicts with the company's own adoption history. Presenting an unrealistic figure damages credibility when results fall short.",
      "Value depends on adoption. A sensitivity analysis shows ROI under conservative, expected, and optimistic adoption. Stating the assumptions lets decision-makers judge the risk.",
      "Benefits come only from employees who use the tool. Removing adoption would make the estimate less accurate, not more.",
      "Inflating costs arbitrarily does not model the adoption risk. It replaces one unrealistic assumption with another."] },

{ id: "d2-32", d: 2,
  q: "A company's IT department drafted an AI strategy on its own. The strategy lists AI tools to adopt but does not reference the company's strategic objectives.\n\nHow should the company revise the AI strategy?",
  o: ["Tie each AI initiative to a strategic objective, and rank each by its business outcome.",
      "Keep the strategy with IT, and communicate the tool list to the business units.",
      "Expand the tool list so that every department receives at least one AI tool.",
      "Benchmark the tool list against the tools that competitors have adopted."],
  a: [0],
  r: ["AI is a means to business outcomes. Each initiative should trace to a strategic objective, such as growth, efficiency, or customer experience, and be prioritized by the value it creates.",
      "An IT-only strategy lacks business ownership and alignment. Communicating a tool list does not connect AI to business outcomes.",
      "Spreading tools across departments is a technology-first approach. It does not ensure that any initiative creates business value.",
      "Competitors' tools reflect their own strategies. Benchmarking tools does not align the strategy with this company's objectives."] },

{ id: "d2-33", d: 2,
  q: "A company's generative AI (GenAI) costs are growing faster than the value that the application delivers. The company wants to cut costs without reducing quality below its target.\n\nWhich actions will MOST effectively reduce costs? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["Use the smallest model that meets the quality target, and route simple requests to it",
      "Reduce tokens with concise prompts, targeted retrieval, and caching of repeated content",
      "Route every request to the largest available model to reduce the number of retries",
      "Turn off cost and usage monitoring to save on the fees for monitoring tools",
      "Send each request to two different models, and keep the better of the two answers"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Right-sizing the model is a key cost lever. Smaller models cost less per token. Routing simple requests to a smaller model keeps quality where it matters.",
      "Token volume drives GenAI cost. Shorter prompts, retrieving only relevant context, and caching repeated content all reduce tokens.",
      "The largest model costs the most per request. The quality target does not require it for every request.",
      "Monitoring shows where costs come from. Without it, the company cannot find or verify savings.",
      "Sending each request to two models roughly doubles inference cost. It works against the goal."] },

{ id: "d2-34", d: 2,
  q: "Two AI proposals have similar projected return on investment (ROI) and similar risk. Proposal 1 automates an internal back-office task. Proposal 2 supports the company's stated strategic priority of improving customer retention.\n\nWhich proposal should the company prioritize?",
  o: ["Proposal 1, because internal projects are always easier to deliver",
      "Proposal 2, because it aligns with the company's strategic priority",
      "Neither proposal, because similar ROI means there is no clear winner",
      "Proposal 1, because back-office savings are easier to report to the board"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Internal projects are not always easier. The scenario states that both proposals have similar risk, so ease of delivery does not separate them.",
      "When value and risk are similar, strategic alignment decides. Proposal 2 directly supports a stated strategic priority, which makes it the stronger choice.",
      "Similar ROI does not mean that the proposals are equal. Strategic alignment is a separate prioritization factor.",
      "Ease of reporting does not create value. The company should prioritize the proposal that advances its strategy."] },

{ id: "d2-35", d: 2,
  q: "An executive wants to approve an AI project because the proof of concept (POC) worked technically. No one has estimated full costs, adoption, or data readiness for production.\n\nWhy is the technical result alone insufficient for approval?",
  o: ["A technically successful POC guarantees a positive return on investment (ROI).",
      "A solution can work technically yet still fail on cost, adoption, risk, or data readiness.",
      "A POC cannot show whether a solution is technically feasible at any scale.",
      "Only the model's accuracy score matters, and the POC did not report an accuracy score."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Technical success does not guarantee ROI. Costs, adoption, and risk can still make a working solution a poor investment.",
      "Technical feasibility is necessary but not sufficient. The business case must also show value that exceeds total cost, acceptable risk, organizational readiness, and fit with strategy.",
      "A POC does show technical feasibility, which is its purpose. The gap is everything that production requires beyond feasibility.",
      "Accuracy is one factor among many. Business approval also depends on value, cost, adoption, and risk."] },

{ id: "d2-36", d: 2,
  q: "A generative AI (GenAI) coding assistant saves developers an average of 5 hours each week. The finance team asks how the time savings translate into business value.\n\nWhich response BEST answers the finance team?",
  o: ["Multiply the hours saved by salary, and report the result as cash savings.",
      "Show how the freed time is redeployed, and measure those outcomes and quality.",
      "Report the number of lines of code that the assistant generates each week.",
      "Explain that productivity tools create value that cannot be measured."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Hours saved become cash savings only if costs actually fall, for example through reduced contractor spending. Otherwise, the salary cost stays the same, and the value depends on how the time is used.",
      "Time saved is a productivity metric. Business value comes from what the capacity produces, such as faster feature delivery or a smaller backlog. Tracking quality ensures that speed does not add defects.",
      "Lines of code measure output volume, not value. More code can even mean more maintenance.",
      "Productivity value can be measured through outcomes such as delivery speed and quality. Claiming that it cannot be measured weakens the business case."] },

{ id: "d2-37", d: 2,
  q: "A company plans to move its customer service AI assistant from one AI platform to another to reduce costs. Customers use the assistant around the clock.\n\nWhich considerations are MOST important before the move? (Select TWO.)",
  o: ["A transition plan, such as a parallel run with a rollback option, to protect continuity",
      "Re-evaluation of quality, latency, cost, data, and integrations on the new platform",
      "A single weekend cutover without parallel testing, so that the savings start sooner",
      "A platform choice based only on the list price per token that each vendor publishes",
      "No communication to stakeholders, because the change affects only the technology"],
  a: [0, 1],
  r: ["Business continuity is a key consideration when moving a process between AI platforms. A parallel run with a rollback option keeps service available if the new platform underperforms.",
      "The exam guide names performance impact, data readiness, and cost implications as migration considerations. Re-evaluating on representative cases confirms that the new platform meets the baseline.",
      "A cutover without testing risks an outage for a service that customers use around the clock. Faster savings do not justify that risk.",
      "List price is only one part of total cost. Quality, latency, and integration effort can outweigh a lower token price.",
      "Service teams and customers can notice changes in behavior. Stakeholders need to know the timeline and the fallback plan."] },

{ id: "d2-38", d: 2,
  q: "In one business line, most competitors already use AI extensively in customer service, and the company has not started. In another business line, AI adoption across the industry is nascent and unproven.\n\nHow should the company set its AI investment level for each business line?",
  o: ["Invest the same amount in both business lines, regardless of each market's maturity",
      "Invest decisively where AI is table stakes, and run staged experiments elsewhere",
      "Invest only in the nascent line to become the first mover in that market",
      "Wait for competitors to publish results in both lines before investing"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["The two markets have very different maturity and competitive dynamics. Identical investment ignores the gap in one line and the uncertainty in the other.",
      "The right investment level depends on industry maturity and competitive dynamics. Where AI is table stakes, falling further behind is a competitive risk. Where AI is nascent, staged experiments limit risk while the company learns.",
      "Ignoring the mature line leaves the company behind competitors that already use AI. First-mover benefits in the nascent line are also uncertain.",
      "Waiting widens the gap in the mature line. In the nascent line, the company learns nothing while it waits."] },

{ id: "d2-39", d: 2,
  q: "A company runs custom machine learning (ML) models on Amazon SageMaker AI. Usage has been steady and predictable for a year and is expected to continue.\n\nWhich AWS cost optimization option is MOST appropriate?",
  o: ["Savings Plans that commit to steady usage for a 1-year or 3-year term",
      "AWS Marketplace listings that offer third-party machine learning models",
      "A switch to seat-based licensing for each data scientist on the ML team",
      "AWS Pricing Calculator estimates of the current monthly model usage"],
  a: [0],
  r: ["Savings Plans lower prices in exchange for a commitment to a consistent amount of usage over 1 or 3 years. Savings Plans are available for SageMaker AI and suit steady, predictable workloads.",
      "AWS Marketplace is for procuring third-party software. It does not reduce the cost of the company's existing SageMaker AI usage.",
      "SageMaker AI is priced by instance usage, not by user. Seat-based licensing is not an option for this workload.",
      "AWS Pricing Calculator estimates costs. It does not lower them."] },

{ id: "d2-40", d: 2,
  q: "A vendor pitches a $2 million enterprise-wide AI deployment to automate claims processing. The company has no baseline metrics for the current process and no defined success key performance indicators (KPIs). The claims data sits in three unconnected systems.\n\nWhat is the BEST assessment of the proposal?",
  o: ["Approve it, because the vendor has a strong reputation in claims processing.",
      "Treat it as premature, and first set KPIs, baselines, and data readiness.",
      "Reject AI for claims processing permanently, because the data is not ready.",
      "Approve it, and ask the vendor to define the success KPIs after launch."],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Vendor reputation does not fix missing baselines, undefined success measures, or fragmented data. These gaps put the investment at risk regardless of the vendor.",
      "Critical success factors are missing. The company should define the problem and KPIs, capture baselines, and fix data readiness, then validate value with a scoped pilot before committing $2 million.",
      "The data problems can be fixed. A permanent rejection discards a potentially valuable use case.",
      "KPIs defined after launch cannot be compared with a baseline. The company would be unable to prove value."] },

{ id: "d2-41", d: 2,
  q: "A team pilots generative AI (GenAI) to draft replies for the accounts payable query inbox. The team wants a metric that shows the pilot's impact on that process.\n\nWhich metric BEST demonstrates the pilot's impact?",
  o: ["Company-wide employee satisfaction measured in the annual survey",
      "Time to resolve payable queries and reply accuracy, against a baseline",
      "The company's total IT spending for the current fiscal year, by department",
      "The number of AI tools that the company has licensed across departments"],
  a: [1],
  r: ["Company-wide satisfaction is influenced by many factors outside the pilot. It cannot isolate the pilot's effect on the accounts payable process.",
      "Key performance indicators (KPIs) should measure the specific process that the AI changes, against a baseline captured before the pilot. Resolution time shows efficiency, and accuracy shows quality.",
      "Total IT spending reflects many projects. It does not show how the pilot changed the accounts payable process.",
      "The number of licensed tools measures purchasing, not the pilot's effect on the process."] },
]);
